AB  - Background. There is a lack of a valid instrument for describing the extent of smoking cessation activities by general practitioners (GPs) from the patients' perspective. Therefore, a short instrument was developed in the context of the Co5mo5 study. The aim of the present study was the psychometric evaluation of the "SmoCess-GP" instrument for smokers.
   Methods. 127 smoking participants of Co5mo5 were included in the analyses. Psychometric evaluation was conducted with exploratory factor and reliability analysis. The construct validity was tested with bivariate correlations.
   Results. The resulting one-dimensional structure of the"SmoCess-GP" measure can explain 64.6% of the overall variance. Cronbach's alpha was.68. With regard to construct validity, a hypothesis-consistent relationship with nicotine dependence was determined (r=.266, p<.01).
   Conclusions. The measure seems to be a useful tool for analysing the extent of GPs' secondary preventive care of smokers. Furthermore, the instrument may provide a checklist for practitioners to guide them through smoking cessation counselling.
