AB  - Recreational events on urban beaches are increasingly recognized as significant drivers of marine litter accumulation, representing challenges to coastal management and environmental sustainability. This study investigates the temporal and spatial variations in marine litter at Mar Grosso Beach, southern Brazil, during four major recreational events: Universipraia, MotoLaguna, New Year's Eve, and Carnival. A photo-identification methodology was employed to assess litter before, during, and after each event. Results showed a marked increase in litter density post-events, with an average of 43.31 (+/- 23.04) items per transect, compared to 29.12 (+/- 18.76) items before events. Spatial analysis revealed significant concentration of waste at specific beach zones, particularly at station P2, which recorded the highest Clean Coast Index (CCI) values, indicating a localized impact. Marine litter composition shifted notably following the events, with higher proportions of packaging-related debris, including aluminum cans, bottle caps, and cigarette butts, materials commonly associated with public gatherings. The CCI further demonstrated a deterioration in beach cleanliness, with a transition from "moderately clean" to "dirty" conditions, particularly in high-traffic zones. The findings underscore the significant environmental pressures exerted by short-term recreational activities on urban beaches, highlighting the need for enhanced waste management strategies, such as increased waste bin deployment, post-event clean-ups, and public engagement initiatives. This study contributes to a better understanding of event-driven litter dynamics and provides evidence for improved coastal management practices aligned with the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) 11 (Sustainable Cities and Communities) and 14 (Life Below Water).
