TI  - Organizing objectives into a hierarchic list with the nominal group method
T2  - EXERCER-LA REVUE FRANCOPHONE DE MEDECINE GENERALE
LA  - French
KW  - SMOKING
AB  - Background. Only smoking cessation is able to slow down the natural course of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Strategy using the minimal advice is relevant and usable in general medicine. Assessment of biomedical risk by spirometry appears to be useful. The primary objective of the SpiFP trial is to assess spirometry associated with minimal advice for smoking cessation. This study raises the question of methodological problems and choice of secondary objectives.
   Aim. To establish by consensus a list sorted in order of priority secondary objectives and methodological problems for SpiFP.
   Method. The nominal group technique (TGN) has been used, identify a list of research questions on which all research team were in agreement. It included four steps: individual production of research questions, recording, discussion and validation and final vote.
   Results. 4 researchers participated in the group during 90 minutes. 24 issues were selected after discussion. Priority items were: is spirometry feasible for general practitioners (GPs) in France? The use of spirometry by GPs. Does spirometry increased the incidence of COPD? How to train GPs? Is spirometry performed by a GP valid? How to randomise the GP?
   Conclusion. The TGN has been achieved in a limited time to get a consensus list of secondary objectives and methodological problems for SpiFP. The team has a list of 24 items defining a prioritized research agenda.
