AB  - With the arrival of mobile technologies, a new paradigm of teaching, learning and assessment has emerged. M-learning enables immediate and creative collaboration among pupils, knowledge exploration and creation, information searching and improved interaction and communication between students and educators. In addition, it permits access to learning at anytime and anywhere and the instant availability of multiple applications for diverse educational purposes. Despite the obvious attraction and benefits of mobile devices, there are undercurrent concerns regarding the potential adverse effects of mobile technologies including nomophobia, social media addiction, virtual bullying and social phubbing. Consequently, this explorative case study investigates whether student teachers are no less vulnerable to these compulsions and how informed are they regarding the potential pearls and perils of mobile technologies for teaching and learning. Adopting a mixed methods approach, this group and location-based case study examines perspectives and habits of fifty-eight second year undergraduate student teachers regarding mobile device attraction, interaction and distraction via an anonymous online survey. Analysis of both closed and open questions indicate that while all respondents use mobile technologies for a diverse range of social and educational purposes daily, only a quarter of them use them to engage with the institution's virtual learning environment (Moodle) or chosen social media and digital news platform (Twitter). While aware and forthcoming with perspectives regarding social etiquette and mobile use, many respondents confess to social phubbing during lectures. Other findings indicate that there are missed opportunities for lecturers and learners to utilise mobile technologies more for teaching, learning and assessment purposes. This study will be of interest to educators who seek greater insight from higher education students regarding how they utilise mobile technologies and how best to exploit their affordances to advance and enhance M-learning in one's teaching practice.
