AB  - The general practitioner (GP) is an essential factor in the management of hypertensive patients, which includes the provision of nutritional-hygienic advices. Our objective was to analyze the characteristics of patients and their GPs relating to this lack of nutritional-hygienic advice.
   Fifty-nine GPs from Paris responded to questionnaires with their files. Their 1192 hypertensive patients aged 25 to 79 years were interviewed by telephone. Analyses based on mixed models with random intercept focused on 'not giving advice' on salt, losing weight, reducing alcohol consumption and physical exercise.
   The doctors provided less guidance to hypertensive patients with low cardiovascular risk and a recent follow-up with little consultation in the year. By contrast, an organization of practice with lengthy consultations, at the rate of health insurance, participation in continuing medical education, as well as the fact that the doctor himself was hypertensive, were associated with more advice. The development of therapeutic education programs could improve this situation by targeting in a privileged manner patients receiving less guidance.
