TI  - Offer exercise, therapy, acupuncture, or antidepressants for chronic primary pain, says NICE
T2  - BMJ-BRITISH MEDICAL JOURNAL
LA  - English
AB  - He said that people who are taking a drug that is not recommended should ask their doctor to review their prescribing as part of shared decision making. ?This could involve agreeing a plan to carry on taking their medicines if they provide benefit at a safe dose and few harms, or support for them to reduce and stop the medicine if possible. When making shared decisions it?s important that any problems associated with withdrawal are discussed and properly tackled,? said Chrisp. The guideline emphasises the importance of healthcare professionals gaining an understanding of how a person?s life affects their pain and how pain affects their life, including their work and leisure time, relationships with family and friends, and sleep. It recommends that a care and support plan should be developed based on the effects of pain on day-to-day activities, as well as a person?s preferences, abilities, and goals. It also highlights the importance of being honest with the person about the uncertainty of the prognosis. Nick Kosky, a consultant psychiatrist at Dorset HealthCare University NHS Foundation Trust and chair of the guideline committee, said, ?Understandably, people with chronic pain expect a clear diagnosis and effective treatment. But its complexity and the fact GPs and specialists alike find chronic pain challenging to manage, means this is often not possible.? Lucy Ryan, a patient representative on the guideline committee, said that the guideline would provide more consistency in treatment and support for people experiencing chronic pain. ?I am pleased that it has highlighted the potential risks of some of the drugs used to treat chronic primary pain as people are sometimes not made aware of these. I am also pleased that the guideline provides alternative treatment choices and that gaps in research have been identified. I hope that this will mean future guidelines will incorporate more options for people managing chronic primary pain following further research,? she said.
   For many commonly prescribed drugs, including paracetamol, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, benzodiazepines, or opioids there is little or no evidence that they make any difference to people?s quality of life, pain, or psychological distress, but they can cause harm, including possible addiction. It is estimated that 1-6% of the population are affected by primary chronic pain. Paul Chrisp, director of the Centre for Guidelines at NICE, said, ?This guideline is clear in highlighting that, based on the evidence, for most people it?s unlikely that any drug treatments for chronic primary pain, other than antidepressants, provide an adequate balance between any benefits they might provide and the risks associated with them.?
