TI  - Mapping the overdose crisis: 6 locations using open medical examiner data
T2  - JAMIA OPEN
LA  - English
KW  - geographic mapping
KW  - datasets as topic
KW  - coroners and medical examiners
KW  - drug overdose
KW  - PUBLIC-HEALTH
KW  - MORTALITY-RATES
KW  - DEATHS
AB  - Objective Medical examiners and coroners (ME/C) oversee medicolegal death investigations which determine causes of death and other contextual factors that may have influenced a death. We utilize open data releases from ME/C offices covering 6 different geographic areas to demonstrate the strengths and limitations of ME/C data for forensic epidemiology research.Materials and Methods We use our novel geoPIPE tool to establish a pipeline that (a) automates ingesting open data releases, (b) geocodes records where possible to yield a spatial component, (c) enhances data with variables useful for overdose research, such as flagging substances contributing to each death, and (d) publishes the enriched data to our open repository. We use results from this pipeline to highlight similarities and differences of overdose data across different sources.Results Text processing to extract drugs contributing to each death yielded compatible data across all locations. Conversely, geospatial analyses are sometimes incompatible due to differences in available geographic resolution, which range from fine-grain latitude and longitude coordinates to larger regions identified by zip codes. Our pipeline pushes weekly results to an open repository.Discussion Open ME/C data are highly useful for research on substance use disorders; our visualizations demonstrate the ability to contextualize overdose data within and across specific geographic regions. Furthermore, the spatial component of our results enables clustering of overdose events and accessibility studies for resources related to preventing overdose deaths.Conclusions Given the utility to public health researchers, we advocate that other ME/C offices explore releasing open data and for policy makers to support and fund transparency efforts.
   Medical examiners and coroners investigate unexpected deaths to determine causes, especially for drug overdoses, and some offices share their findings publicly as open data. However, this information can be difficult for researchers to use because each office formats their data differently and publishes it in a raw form with text fields that require additional processing to use. We created a tool called geoPIPE that automatically collects death investigation data from 6 different locations, standardizes it, and adds useful information like identifying which drugs caused each death and mapping where deaths occurred, and finally makes this processed data freely available to other researchers. The tool successfully identified drugs involved in overdose deaths in all our locations, making it possible to compare overdose patterns between different areas. This standardized, publicly available overdose data help researchers and public health officials better understand drug overdose patterns in their communities. The geographic information supports both the creation of overdose hotspot maps and can identify where prevention resources are most needed. We encourage more medical examiner offices to share their data publicly to support these life-saving efforts.
