AB  - Background. Among opiate substitution treatments, high dosage buprenorphine (HDB) is the most used in France. Prescriptions are mostly done by general practitioners (GP) but only a minority of them are involved in it: some GPs prescribe it often and others only occasionally.
   Objectives. To characterize the prescription of high dosage buprenorphine (HDB) by general practitioner (GP) with regards to the number of their patients using this drug.
   Methods. Retrospective, cross-sectional retrospective analysis of HDB dispensed by pharmacists and reimbursed by the Health insurance of Charente-Maritime during one month, for 2 consecutive years (n=2 845). The clientele was defined by the number of different patients taking HDB for each GP.
   Results. The data related to 1836 unique patients. The greater the clientele with HDB was, the more the GP prescribed high dosage and associations with benzodiazepines. GPs with less than 5 patients with HDB delivered less generic drugs than the others.
   Conclusion. A convergence appears between a high dose of HDB, the association with benzodiazepines and the dispending of the brand-name HDB for the small number of GP getting the largest part of patients with HDB.
