AB  - Due to the increasing awareness of the general population, both the prevalence and incidence of respiratory sleep disorders is continuously increasing as well as the demand for specialist diagnosis, only in small part satisfied due to present structural and operational limitations.
   Aim: To implement an organizational model for the nocturnal cardiorespiratory monitoring of respiratory sleep disorders in the subjects' home, and assess its efficacy, appropriateness, and economic convenience.
   Methods: All subjects who underwent nocturnal cardiorespiratory monitoring at home in the period December 2009-December 2010 were included, and were divided into different groups according to their apnea/hypopnea index (AHI). Statistics: Analysis of variance (ANOVA) for groups, assuming p < 0.05.
   Results: Nocturnal recordings of 461 subjects were evaluated (mean age 58.8 years +/- 13.0 sd; mean body mass index (BMI) = 31.0 kg/m(2) +/- 7.5 sd; n = 351 males). Fifty-four subjects proved normal, while 57 were defined as "normalised" because already treated with continuous positive airways pressure (CPAP). Moreover, 119 subjects showed mild, 113 moderate, and 132 severe respiratory disorders during sleep. Males prevailed (76.1%), particularly in groups with the highest AHI. Although normal subjects were the youngest, no significant differences were seen in the other groups in terms of age and BMI (p = ns). Cardiovascular, but not respiratory, comorbidities proved directly related to AHI severity. Active smoking was unrelated to the AHI score. The nocturnal cardiorespiratory monitoring proved pathological in 88.3% of subjects, and thus its prescription was regarded as appropriate in clinical terms, pathological recordings being more frequently found in subjects prescribed it by specialists rather than by GPs in all groups (p < 0.03). The home model enabled a reduction in the cost of each test by 34.70/0, which corresponded to an overall savings of (sic) 25,456 for the year. A further advantage of the home model was that it enabled a higher number of monitorings to be carried out than would otherwise have been possible within the same timespan and reduced the need for hospitalization (only 9 were required), freeing beds for regular patient admissions.
   Conclusions: The model, based on the nocturnal cardiorespiratory monitoring of sleep disorders in the subject's home, proved effective, appropriate (also in terms of prescription by GPs), relatively cheap, and able to respond to the ever increasing demand for this kind of diagnostic test.
